(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-rates-disease-measurement-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Rates and Disease Measurement Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What example is given for monitoring changes in an infectious agent?","choices":["A restaurant worker with hepatitis A","Foodborne gastrointestinal disease","Resistance emerging to an antibiotic it was once susceptible to","A person with tuberculosis flying internationally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and it matters because the organism has not changed its name while the treatment that works against it has changed entirely. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How does prevalence help in clinical reasoning?","choices":["It establishes the cause of the condition","It determines the severity of the condition","It prioritizes the differential diagnosis","It identifies which test is most accurate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: think horses, not zebras. It also affects the interpretation of test results, because it sets the pretest probability. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What distinguishes cumulative incidence from incidence density?","choices":["Cumulative incidence uses person-time in the denominator","Incidence density counts existing as well as new cases","Cumulative incidence applies only to acute illness","Cumulative incidence uses a group of fixed size"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, whereas incidence density measures new cases in a changing population, which is why its denominator is person-time rather than people. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What is the cost of narrowing the population under study?","choices":["It always introduces measurement error","It may curtail generalisability","It makes the rate impossible to calculate","It converts the study into an experiment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the trade is often worth making, as when a study is limited to those in a service region who need the service, but the limit has to be acknowledged. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What does prevalence set when a test result is interpreted?","choices":["The pretest probability","The sensitivity of the test","The specificity of the test","The reliability of the measurement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and this is why the same result means different things in different settings: a positive test in a population where the disease is rare is far more often a false positive. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is incidence density useful for?","choices":["Populations of fixed size followed together","Dynamic populations, with people moving in and out","Measuring how many people have a condition now","Comparing mortality between nations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is often used in clinical trials, where participants enroll and leave at different times rather than all being present throughout. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What principle underlies years of potential life lost?","choices":["Deaths should be weighted by the cost of treatment","Death at a younger age loses more productive years","All deaths carry equal weight regardless of age","Only deaths from preventable causes are counted"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the practical use follows: it helps target interventions at the causes that take the most life from a population, not merely the most lives. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In chronic disease, how do incidence and prevalence usually compare?","choices":["Incidence is usually higher than prevalence","They are usually equal","Incidence is usually lower than prevalence","Neither can be measured in chronic disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because newly diagnosed patients are added to a group that does not shed its existing members, so cases accumulate. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does the crude mortality rate measure?","choices":["Deaths from all causes in a population","Deaths from one specified cause in a population","Deaths within a particular age group","Deaths among those diagnosed with a disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why it is called crude: it makes no adjustment for the age structure of the population, and an older population will show a higher figure for that reason alone. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does prevalence describe?","choices":["The portion who develop a condition over a period","The portion of a group with a condition at one time","The proportion of those diagnosed who die","The number of deaths per unit of population"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the useful image is a snapshot: it is measured by counting who does and does not have the condition, which is what surveys are good at. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What distinguishes point from period prevalence?","choices":["Point prevalence uses the same calendar date for all","Period prevalence counts only new cases","Point prevalence is a single moment for each person","Point prevalence applies only to chronic disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and that moment need not be the same calendar date for everyone. Period prevalence counts cases present during a specified stretch of time. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which measure is commonly used to compare health status between nations?","choices":["Infant mortality rate","Crude death rate","Proportionate mortality","Death-to-case ratio"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: deaths among children under one year divided by live births in the same period, multiplied by one thousand. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which is a named surveillance strategy?","choices":["Reviewing published systematic reviews","Randomly assigning exposures in the population","Pooling data from several published studies","Screening high-risk persons or groups"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside clinical symptoms and syndromes, mortality data, provider reporting of morbidity and laboratory reporting of positive results. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A town of 1,000 has 80 cases of a disease in 2015, and 20 previously healthy people develop it between 2016 and 2018. Which figure is the incidence for that period?","choices":["Eight percent","Ten percent","Eighteen percent","Two percent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, since 20 new cases in 1,000 people is the new-case count over the period. The 100 cases present in 2018 give a prevalence of ten percent. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does endemic mean?","choices":["A concentration of new cases in a given time","Disease limited by geographic location","Widespread cases across many regions","A disease that has been eradicated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it belongs to distribution by place, alongside the outbreak lists, travel notices and food safety recalls maintained nationally. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"On which three levels is the distribution of disease evaluated?","choices":["Agent, host and environment","Person, place and time","Incidence, prevalence and mortality","Nominal, ordinal and interval"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the work of looking for patterns across those three is a large part of what epidemiologists actually do. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which age range defines the neonatal mortality rate?","choices":["28 to 364 days","Under one year","Under 28 days","Under five years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with live births as the denominator. Postneonatal mortality covers 28 to 364 days, and infant mortality covers the whole first year. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the 1976 Philadelphia Legionnaires' disease outbreak illustrate?","choices":["A slowly developing pandemic","An endemic disease confined by geography","A prevalence survey of a community","An epidemic curve tied to a single gathering"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: cases were plotted by date of onset across July and August, following an American Legion convention. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What does case fatality measure?","choices":["Disease frequency in a population","The distribution of deaths among causes","Years of productive life lost to early death","Disease severity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, expressed as the proportion of people diagnosed with a disease who die from it, which says nothing about how common the disease is. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which is an epidemiologic function of surveillance?","choices":["Detecting outbreaks and epidemics","Assigning treatment to individual patients","Publishing systematic reviews of the literature","Setting the price of preventive services"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside determining the magnitude of a problem, understanding natural history, documenting geographic spread, testing etiologic hypotheses, evaluating control strategies and monitoring changes in an agent. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Where does the potential for error lie in incidence density?","choices":["In variation in duration of follow-up","In counting existing cases as new ones","In the population being of fixed size","In the multiplier used to express the result"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because person-time treats one person followed ten years as equivalent to ten people followed one year, which may not be true of the risk involved. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"At which levels does disease surveillance operate?","choices":["National level only","National, state and county","Hospital level only","International level only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it may be active or passive. Its first contribution is a baseline, without which no rise in cases can be recognized as a rise. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is an epidemic curve?","choices":["The number of cases plotted against location","The proportion of cases that prove fatal","The number of cases plotted against time","The cumulative attack rate across subgroups"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and its shape carries information about the type of exposure, the route of spread and when exposure occurred. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which characteristics of an outbreak are described alongside the epidemic curve?","choices":["Case fatality, years of life lost and attack rate","Incidence, prevalence and mortality","Type of exposure, route of spread and timing of exposure","Person, place and time"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and together they feed the hypotheses about source, type and route of spread that the investigation then tests. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why may prevalence be lower than incidence in acute illness?","choices":["People recover, so few are ill at any one moment","Acute illnesses are diagnosed less often","Acute illnesses have no measurable incidence","Acute illness is not counted in prevalence studies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with the common cold as the example: a great many people are affected over a period while only a small number have it at any given instant. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is another name for an incidence study?","choices":["A cross-sectional study","A case series","A meta-analysis","A cohort study"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because a population is followed over time to identify whether the condition appears, which is what following a cohort means. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What distinguishes an epidemic from a pandemic?","choices":["An epidemic is widespread; a pandemic is local","An epidemic is limited by geography","A pandemic always develops rapidly","An epidemic concentrates new cases in time; a pandemic is widespread"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and a pandemic may develop rapidly, as with the influenza outbreak of the early 1900s, or slowly, as with HIV and AIDS. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What sits in the denominator of the crude death rate?","choices":["The total number of deaths from all causes","The mid-interval population","The number of live births in the same interval","The number of new cases of the disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it is multiplied by 1,000 or 100,000 so the result is a legible number rather than a small decimal. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What effect does awareness of an epidemic have?","choices":["It can make new cases easier to diagnose","It reduces the true incidence of the disease","It converts an epidemic into a pandemic","It has no effect on case finding"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with early COVID-19 as the example. It is also a reason measured frequency can rise partly because clinicians started looking. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the death-to-case ratio compare?","choices":["Deaths from a cause against new cases of it","Deaths from a cause against the whole population","Deaths from a cause against all deaths","Deaths against live births"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, both over the same time interval, multiplied by one hundred. It measures how lethal a disease is rather than how much of it there is. — 3 PAJ5512 Rates and Measurement MBF_2026sc.pptx, Slide 9"}]};
